It sounds like you're looking for open population models, which are implemented in unmarked with the pcountopen function.
Some papers to get you started:
- Dail & Madsen 2011 <https://sci-hub.tw/https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1541-0420.2010.01465.x>
- Hostetler and Chandler 2015 <https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdf/10.1890/14-1487.1?casa_token=nmFbNqRiO98AAAAA:O3Z2v4P2lGHPOdZY7JASwJ7E5Go4vpc_yTJr2LHIlnX2ru5YKJ88azWfSJqxVE6Qd2UxIwt1SLp3w84>
- Bellier, Kéry, and Schaub 2016 <https://sci-hub.tw/https://doi.org/10.1111/2041-210X.12572>
- Zhao, Royle, and Boomer 2017 <https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s10144-017-0600-7.pdf>
- and a reminder that these models can be pretty sensitive to model assumptions: Duarte, Adams, and Peterson 2018 <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304380018300607/pdfft?casa_token=d8foSARhaLwAAAAA:lEGlCqaHYuwrfeMWDQ-C9sfbRfhU7J3TC3UpxY4lIFE5VFaUi6F0eQOCWCVQir0GVtxNLB2jRQ&md5=01f6f53db8c905a4653bc5a5797f9b08&pid=1-s2.0-S0304380018300607-main.pdf> , Monroe et al. 2019 <https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1002/ecs2.2791> or Zhao and Royle 2019 <https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304380018304186/pdfft?casa_token=7MncqkF94_MAAAAA:GgCrHgEVbuH0SZ3rYnMp1N63PtX0pVlsJhhye_QoCAUJ6KtpoW5nxjo1APkhFUKvOr6ry-ed4g&md5=a2d5c398759ef4050b9c049c75e98335&pid=1-s2.0-S0304380018304186-main.pdf>
- The soon(?) to be released volume 2 <https://www.elsevier.com/books/applied-hierarchical-modeling-in-ecology-analysis-of-distribution-abundance-and-species-richness-in-r-and-bugs/kery/978-0-12-809585-0> of Applied Hierarchical Modeling in Ecology

