Someone correct me I'm wrong, but I don't think occupancy models that account for availability are incorporated into the main software packages. Folks have written their own models (e.g. 1 <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2664.2010.01921.x>, 2 <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biocon.2019.108246>), so you could go that (more difficult) route. It would also be helpful to know more about how you collected your data. What was the sampling design? How did you estimate detection probability and availability rate?

For what it's worth, gdistsamp, gmultmix, and gpcount are all versions of N-mixture models (for abundance, not presence) that are intended to address some forms of availability. occuMS allows for different classes of animals (e.g. breeding vs. non-breeding), but you'd still need to be able to detect organisms in each of the classes in order to use the model.

Philip Shirk

